```python
correlation(
    data,
    variables=None,
    year=None,
    *,
    method='pearson',
    digits=2,
    title=None,
    ref=None,
)
```

Correlations between the variables of a panel in one year.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name      | Type        | Description                                                                                                    | Default     |
|-----------|-------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------|
| data      |             | A dataset id, or a panel with ``geo_id`` (and ``year``), e.g. from ``load``                                    | _required_  |
| variables |             | The numeric columns to correlate (default: every numeric variable; ids and the year are left out)              | `None`      |
| year      |             | The year to use (default: the last with data for the first variable), or ``"all"`` to pool every year          | `None`      |
| method    | str         | ``"pearson"`` (linear), ``"spearman"`` (ranks) or ``"kendall"``: the coefficient of the matrix and the heatmap | `'pearson'` |
| digits    | int         | Decimals written in the heatmap and the table                                                                  | `2`         |
| title     | str \| None | The figure and table title                                                                                     | `None`      |

## Notes {.doc-section .doc-section-notes}

Only the observations with data for every variable are used, so that all the
coefficients refer to the same units.

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type              | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |
|--------|-------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | CorrelationResult | A result with ``.df`` (the correlation matrix), ``.pairs`` (one row per pair, from the strongest positive to the strongest negative), ``.fig`` (a heatmap centred at zero with the coefficients in the cells), ``.gt`` (a table with Pearson above the diagonal and Spearman below), ``.n`` (observations), ``.variables``, ``.year``, ``.notes`` and ``.interpret()``. |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q geo4bolivia
import geo4bolivia as geo

res = geo.correlation("sdg01", year=2024)
print(res.interpret())
res.fig
```


::: {#c853bfe6 .cell execution_count=1}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
Pearson correlations between 5 variables in 2024, on the 343 observations with data for all of them.

The strongest positive correlation is between **Multidimensional poverty index (0-1)** and **Unsatisfied basic needs (% of population)** (r = 0.82): a strong association, in which units with more of one tend to have more of the other.

The strongest negative correlation is between **No poverty index (0-100)** and **Unsatisfied basic needs (% of population)** (r = -0.94): a strong association, in which units with more of one tend to have less of the other.

The weakest correlation, in either direction, is between **Energy poverty (% of dwellings)** and **Access to the 3 basic services (% of households)** (r = -0.46).

A coefficient near +1 or -1 means two variables move together closely, in the same or in opposite directions; near 0, that knowing one says little about the other. The table (`.gt`) sets Pearson correlations (above the diagonal) against Spearman rank correlations (below): a large gap between the two points to a non-linear relation or to outliers.

_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._
:::

